Two Lines, One Day (两条线，一天): credits

An unofficial fan film. It is not made, endorsed or checked by Deutsche Bahn, DB InfraGO, DELFI e.V. or gtfs.de, and
shows no operator logo. Train line names (ICE 43, IC 55 ...), station names and times are public timetable facts.

Timetable: gtfs.de "fv_free" feed (Schienenfernverkehr Deutschland, German long-distance rail), published by gtfs.de,
  data provided by DELFI e.V. (feed_info: "gtfs.de - GTFS für Deutschland, Daten bereitgestellt von DELFI e.V.").
  Licence: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 (CC BY 4.0), as stated on https://gtfs.de/de/feeds/de_fv/ (checked 2026-11-13).
  https://download.gtfs.de/germany/fv_free/latest.zip, downloaded 2026-11-13 (calendar 2026-11-07 to 2026-12-07).
  The film draws the service day Wednesday 18 November 2026: every trip of that day that runs along the two lines below
  (179 train paths). All times are the feed's published arrival and departure times; none is interpolated. Between two
  published stops a train is drawn as a straight line, so where two lines cross between stations is approximate.
  The feed holds long-distance trains only: no regional and no freight trains are drawn (said on the last card).
  Trips are the feed's service day 18 Nov; trips of 17 Nov still running after midnight are not on the sheet.
  The trimmed file the film draws is data/graph.json, made by tools/prep.py (via neo/geo/gtfs.py).

Map data: (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, Open Database License (ODbL) 1.0. https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright
  Fetched 2026-11-13 through the Overpass API (neo/geo/osm.py's fetch): railway=rail ways (no service tracks) and the
  centre lines of the Rhine, Main, Mosel, Lahn, Sieg, Nahe, Ahr, Wied, Agger, Aar and Wisper, in 6.85-8.75 E,
  49.95-50.98 N. tools/prep.py finds the two lines on that network (shortest path through the stations), measures them
  in kilometres and keeps them, their tunnels and the rivers near them in data/graph.json, which is a derived database
  and stays under ODbL 1.0. The film is a produced work: it credits OpenStreetMap on its last card.
  View: the two lines and the rivers within 9 km of them, first as they lie on the ground (north up), then straightened
  into the distance axis. No national, regional or other boundary line is fetched or drawn.
  Stations are placed from the GTFS stop positions, projected onto the OSM line (gtfs.de lists OpenStreetMap
  contributors among the feed's attributions).

Picture: drawn entirely in code (Canvas 2D). No images, video or 3D models are used.
Sound: the score (music box, harp and pizzicato bass by the library's core/audio/pluck.py; reed drone, clock tick,
  horn, rail joints and drafting foley synthesized in mix.py) and the ambience are synthesized. No samples or
  recordings are used.

